Q: CSS variables use in Vue A: <p>Why not just use this?</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style scoped&gt;
  * {
    --var-txt-color: #c1d32f;
  }
&lt;/style&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The generated CSS is:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>*[data-v-d235d782] {
  --var-txt-color: #c1d32f;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This has been working for me.</p>
<p>I just discovered that it looks like this also works, using the <code>deep selector</code> in <a href="https://vue-loader.vuejs.org/guide/scoped-css.html#deep-selectors" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Vue 2</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;  {
  --var-txt-color: #c1d32f;
}
</code></pre>
<p>or <a href="https://vuejs.org/api/sfc-css-features.html#deep-selectors" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Vue 3</a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>:deep()  {
  --var-txt-color: #c1d32f;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Generated CSS is:</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>[data-v-d235d782] {
  --var-txt-color: #c1d32f;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I think I like this method more.</p>
